WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.196 --> 00:00:01.564
- If you look everywhere around you,

00:00:01.564 --> 00:00:03.219
it's kinda what James Brown said,

00:00:03.219 --> 00:00:05.424
is that "Every piece of music after him

00:00:05.424 --> 00:00:07.312
"you could hear a little
bit 'a James Brown."

00:00:07.312 --> 00:00:09.941
That's, like, Black History in a nutshell.

00:00:09.941 --> 00:00:10.758
(whoosh)

00:00:10.758 --> 00:00:11.511
(swish)

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.641
(creak)

00:00:12.641 --> 00:00:15.284
(emotional piano music)

00:00:15.284 --> 00:00:16.682
- And I think black
history is so important

00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:19.910
because it's kind of your
time to be celebrated.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.770
- There should be a
month where we celebrate

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.697
amazing leaders that fought
for freedom for everybody.

00:00:24.697 --> 00:00:26.452
- It gives a more complete picture

00:00:26.452 --> 00:00:28.492
of the history of the United States.

00:00:28.492 --> 00:00:31.118
- Black History Month
means people can overcome

00:00:31.118 --> 00:00:34.310
a lot of sad, oppressive
things and come out

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:37.340
being big and beautiful
and shine right through.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.279
- It's a story about civil disobedience.

00:00:39.279 --> 00:00:42.194
It's about people grabbing power.

00:00:42.194 --> 00:00:44.758
- The first book I ever
read where I was like,

00:00:44.758 --> 00:00:47.649
I relate to this so much, was
Black Boy by Richard Wright.

00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:49.872
It was about poverty, and struggle,

00:00:49.872 --> 00:00:52.096
and tryin' to overcome obstacles,

00:00:52.096 --> 00:00:53.684
and that was the first
time I was like "Wow."

00:00:53.684 --> 00:00:56.122
- Learning about hip-hop when I was young,

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:59.640
graffiti, MCing, DJing, breaking.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.068
Without that I wouldn't
be who I was today.

00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:05.682
- Black folk in America
came over in ships.

00:01:05.682 --> 00:01:07.197
Look at the way that you came in.

00:01:07.197 --> 00:01:08.948
But you look at yourself
now and you're like,

00:01:08.948 --> 00:01:11.359
whoa, we are fucking blowing up.

00:01:11.359 --> 00:01:14.714
- Do I listen to those stories
and identify with them,

00:01:14.714 --> 00:01:16.739
regardless of what color I am?

00:01:16.739 --> 00:01:17.390
Yeah!

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.216
And I think it's important for
everyone to hear this stuff.

00:01:20.216 --> 00:01:22.489
- I think of black leaders
such as Angela Davis.

00:01:22.489 --> 00:01:24.465
I admire them, I read all their books.

00:01:24.465 --> 00:01:26.335
- And Latinos, like,
we're crossing rivers,

00:01:26.335 --> 00:01:27.939
and we're crossing borders,
and we're in the back

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:29.576
of cars, sometimes, to get here.

00:01:29.576 --> 00:01:31.568
And it's almost like, whoa, look at them.

00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:32.741
We can do it too.

00:01:32.741 --> 00:01:35.308
- Just close your eyes,
and listen to the stories,

00:01:35.308 --> 00:01:38.718
and you will relate because
they're American stories.

00:01:43.358 --> 00:01:43.901
(swoosh)

00:01:43.901 --> 00:01:44.456
(swish)

00:01:44.456 --> 00:01:45.059
(whoosh)

00:01:45.059 --> 00:01:46.187
(creak)

